To ensure this works on the first try, we will use a Node.js application as the example. Below is the complete file structure and the code for every file required in your repository.

Project File Structure
Place these files in your root directory in Visual Studio:

Plaintext
/my-web-app
├── app.js               # The web application logic
├── package.json         # Node.js dependencies
├── Dockerfile           # Instructions for Docker image
├── k8s-deployment.yaml  # Kubernetes cluster configuration
└── Jenkinsfile          # Jenkins Pipeline-as-Code
1. The Web Application (app.js)
This includes the requested Degree Certificate details so it shows up immediately upon deployment.

JavaScript

const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const PORT = 80;

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
    res.send(`
        <div style="text-align: center; border: 10px double #555; padding: 50px; font-family: serif;">
            <h1>STUDENT DEGREE CERTIFICATE</h1>
            <p>This is to certify that the automation process was successful.</p>
            <hr width="50%">
            <h2>Affiliation Details</h2>
            <p><b>University:</b> Your University Name</p>
            <p><b>Department:</b> Computer Science & Engineering</p>
            <p><b>Accreditation:</b> AICTE / UGC Affiliated</p>
            <p><b>Status:</b> CI/CD Pipeline Deployment Verified</p>
        </div>
    `);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`App running on port ${PORT}`);
});



