3. Docker Configuration (Dockerfile)
This file tells Jenkins how to package your application.

Dockerfile
# Use lightweight Node image
FROM node:18-alpine

# Create app directory
WORKDIR /usr/src/app

# Install dependencies
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install

# Bundle app source
COPY . .

# Expose port 80 and start
EXPOSE 80
CMD [ "node", "app.js" ]



4. Kubernetes Configuration (k8s-deployment.yaml)
This creates the Cluster and the NodePort to view the application.

YAML
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-app-deployment
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: certificate-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: certificate-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web-app
        image: YOUR_DOCKERHUB_USERNAME/certificate-app:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-app-service
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: certificate-app
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30007




5. Jenkins Pipeline (Jenkinsfile)
This automates the entire process. Copy this into the "Pipeline Script" section of your Jenkins job or keep it in the root folder.

Groovy
pipeline {
    agent any

    environment {
        // Change these to your actual DockerHub username
        DOCKER_HUB_USER = 'your-dockerhub-username'
        IMAGE_NAME = 'certificate-app'
    }

    stages {
        stage('Checkout GITHUB') {
            steps {
                // Jenkins will automatically checkout if configured via 'Pipeline from SCM'
                // Otherwise, use: checkout scm
                git 'https://github.com/YOUR_GITHUB_USER/YOUR_REPO.git'
            }
        }

        stage('Create Docker image') {
            steps {
                sh "docker build -t ${DOCKER_HUB_USER}/${IMAGE_NAME}:latest ."
            }
        }

        stage('Push to Docker Hub') {
            steps {
                // Ensure you created 'docker-hub-creds' in Jenkins -> Manage Jenkins -> Credentials
                withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: 'docker-hub-creds', passwordVariable: 'PASS', usernameVariable: 'USER')]) {
                    sh "echo \$PASS | docker login -u \$USER --password-stdin"
                    sh "docker push ${DOCKER_HUB_USER}/${IMAGE_NAME}:latest"
                }
            }
        }

        stage('Create Cluster in Kubernetes') {
            steps {
                // Ensure 'kubeconfig' is added to Jenkins Credentials as a 'Secret File'
                withCredentials([file(credentialsId: 'kubeconfig', variable: 'KUBECONFIG')]) {
                    sh "kubectl --kubeconfig=\$KUBECONFIG apply -f k8s-deployment.yaml"
                    sh "kubectl --kubeconfig=\$KUBECONFIG rollout restart deployment/web-app-deployment"
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
Deployment Flow Diagram
How to run this "First Try":
GitHub: Create a repo and push all these files.

Docker Hub: Create a public repository named certificate-app.

Jenkins Credentials:

Add Username with Password for Docker Hub (ID: docker-hub-creds).

Add Secret File for your Kubernetes config (ID: kubeconfig).

Jenkins Job: Create a "Pipeline" job, point it to your GitHub URL, and click Build Now.

View Results: Go to http://<Your-Node-IP>:30007 to see your certificate.



3. Create and Run the Pipeline Job


This is where the automation comes to life.

On the Jenkins Dashboard, click New Item.

Enter name: Certificate-Deployment and select Pipeline, then click OK.

Scroll down to the Pipeline section:

Definition: Pipeline script from SCM

SCM: Git

Repositories: Paste your GitHub Repo URL

Branch: */main (or */master)

Click Save.

Click Build Now.

4. Verification (The Final Result)

Once the pipeline finishes (all stages turn green), you need to prove the application is live.

Check the Cluster: In your terminal, run:

Bash
kubectl get pods
kubectl get services
Ensure web-app-service shows port 80:30007/TCP.

Access the App: Open your browser and type:
http://<YOUR_KUBERNETES_NODE_IP>:30007

Note: If you are using Minikube, run minikube ip to get the IP. If you are on a cloud provider (AWS/Azure), ensure port 30007 is open in your Security Groups/Firewall.
